/* book exam traps: Using total pressure P in pA = H xA instead of the solute partial pressure pA = yAP. | Mixing an H quoted in bar·m³/kmol with a mole-fraction calculation; the two Henry forms need different H values. | Forgetting that H changes with the form chosen, so a bare number for H without units or form is meaningless. | Saying total pressure changes H. H depends on solute, solvent and temperature; total pressure changes pA = yP and m = H/P, and so the equilibrium x. | Applying Henry's law to a concentrated solution or to the total gas taken up by a reacting solvent (NH₃, SO₂ or CO₂ in water); the law describes only the dilute, physically dissolved part. */
