/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Use mole ratios, not mole fractions, when you draw the lines.
   - Y is the gas mole ratio at any point; Yout is the gas mole ratio leaving the top; X is the liquid mole ratio at that point; Xin is the liquid mole ratio entering the top; L/G is kmol solute-free solvent per kmol inert gas.
   - Yin is the gas mole ratio entering the bottom; Xout is the liquid mole ratio leaving the bottom.
   - The equilibrium line comes from solubility.
   - Start with the two terminal gas compositions and the two terminal liquid compositions.
   - For dilute gases, y ≈ Y and x ≈ X.
   - On the Y–X plot, the operating line is fixed by the flows and the required separation.
   - At the top of an absorber the gas is lean, so Yout is small.
*/
